nt_states are from release 05.0 and beyond, so those are where the active work is concentrated.\n\nLooking at the git log to verify the SRD mappings, I notice there's a discrepancy—srd053 appears to be logname rather than sort, which means I need to reconcile what's actually been implemented versus what's listed in the requirements. I'm noticing a discrepancy where srd053 is labeled as \"srd053-sort\" in the requirement_states, but the recent commits indicate srd053 is actually logname work. The constraint says I should only propose tasks for R-items with \"ready\" status, and all items in requirement_states show \"ready\" — but I need to reconcile this naming mismatch before proceeding. I'm seeing a mismatch between the SRD references in the commits and what's actually in requirement_states—logname shows up as srd053-logname in the commit messages, but requirement_states has srd053-sort instead, and these appear to be different utilities altogether based on the roadmap. Rather than getting caught up in the naming discrepancy, I should just work with what requirement_states actually contains as my source of truth for what's ready. Now I need to find the earliest release with incomplete items, keeping in mind I can only propose tasks for R-items that have a \"ready\" status in requirement_states. I need to verify which SRDs are actually listed in requirement_states before proposing any tasks, since the constraint requires R-items to have \"ready\" status. Let me cross-reference the SRDs that appear in requirement_states against the release roadmap to make sure I'm not proposing work for SRDs that aren't being tracked there. Now I'm mapping out which SRDs correspond to each release based on the roadmap. Release 05.0 has four commands with all their requirements ready, while 04.1 includes five commands where most are complete except logname still has active work. For 04.0, I notice ls doesn't have an SRD entry in the requirement states even though the code status shows